{"name":"Coral Candy","hex":"#f5d0c9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many species of marine invertebrates in the class Anthozoa, most of which build hard calcium carbonate skeletons and form colonies, or a colony belonging to one of those species.","A hard substance made of the skeletons of these organisms."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of coral.","Having the orange-pink color of coral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coral"},{"term":"candy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Crystallized sugar formed by boiling down sugar syrup.","Edible, sweet-tasting confectionery containing sugar, or sometimes artificial sweeteners, and often flavored with fruit, chocolate, nuts, herbs and spices, or artificial flavors."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cook in or coat with sugar syrup.","To form into congelations or crystals, especially of sugar or syrup."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/candy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coral","blocks":[{"text":"Corals are colonial marine invertebrates within the subphylum Anthozoa of the phylum Cnidaria. They typically form compact colonies of many identical individual polyps. Coral species include the important reef builders that inhabit tropical oceans and secrete calcium carbonate to form a hard skeleton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg/330px-Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral","wikidata":"Q171446"},{"title":"Candy","blocks":[{"text":"Candy, also known as sweets and lollies, is a confection that features sugar as a principal ingredient. The category, sugar confectionery, encompasses any sweet confection, including chocolate or chewing gum. Sugar-glazed vegetables, fruit, or nuts are considered candied."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Candy_in_Damascus.jpg/330px-Candy_in_Damascus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candy","wikidata":"Q185583"},{"title":"Coral (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The various tones of the color coral are orange, red and pink representations of the colors of those cnidarians known as precious corals."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_(color)","wikidata":"Q2411228"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Coral_polyp.jpg/330px-Coral_polyp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Coral_polyp.jpg/330px-Coral_polyp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anatomy of a stony coral polyp","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoral_polyp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg/500px-Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg/1280px-Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg/500px-Montastrea_cavernosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Montastraea cavernosa polyps with tentacles extended","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMontastrea_cavernosa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/The_general_organization_of_the_scleractinian_coral_soft_tissue_and_underlying_skeleton.png/500px-The_general_organization_of_the_scleractinian_coral_soft_tissue_and_underlying_skeleton.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, h